# Stage id -> the AP tables dictionary:
#   templates            (ROM offset, vanilla template count, template capacity), 8 bytes each
#   spawn_points         ROM offset of the spawn points, 8 bytes each (x, y, u16 template)
#   spawn_point_capacity entries for the spawn points table
#   all_spawn_points     vanilla's whole spawn points table: (x, y, template). Includes pickups, doors and other non enemies
#   preload              ROM (offset, capacity) of the preload table, capacity being how many rows fit.
#   made_here            species that are placed by something other than a spawn point.
#
#   enemy_templates      template index -> (flag, id, work0, work1, attr, mettaurID, virusID)
#                        The first flag is modified in AP. 
#                        bit 0: respawn after the enemy is deleted. 
#                        AP adds bit 6 (0x40) for a pack's extra enemies 
#                        and bit 7 (0x80) for a moved Pillar Cannon.
#                        work0 and work1 are variables that mean different things for each enemy.
#                        attr represents various other enemy defining traits
#                        mettaurID and virusID is a variable that tells what the enemy should change to in mettaur and virus mode.
#
#   enemy_spawn_points   Any spot in the level that can potentially spawn an enemy.
#                        (spawn point index, template index, x, y in metatiles, positions): 
#
#                        Each position is: 
#                        (dx, dy, the enemy types that can be spawned [floor, air, etc], clearance below, above)
#
#                        For example one enemy spawn point can have many positions:
#                        (3, 8, 123, 106, ((0, 0, ('EMBEDDED',), 0, 12), (0, -1, ('FLOOR',), 0, 12), (0, -2, ('AIR',), 1, 12), (-2, -1, ('SLOPE',), 0, 12)), ((-1,  …
#                        Meaning that this specific spawn point has four enemy spawn positions. 
#                        An embedded one (vanilla), a floor one (one tile up), air one (two tiles up off the ground), and slope one, all with 12 empty spots above it. 
#                        The one at 0,0 coordinates is the vanilla spawn point.
#
#   preload_rows         vanilla rows: (motion, area mask, tile, palette, unk_05, unk_06, cyber)
